{-
   What a conversion will not do, and what `::` is not.

   Four refusals over one rule - `doc/spec/overloading.md`'s Implicit conversion, and
   `compiler/resolve_mir/convert.cpp`, which is the one place any of this is decided. Every case
   below has a `Widen` or a `Narrow` instance somewhere near it, which is the point: what is asserted
   is *which* of the two relates a pair and what happens when it is the lossy one, not that some
   conversion is missing.

   The positive side is `resolve/Convert.yana`, which used to be compiled by both stages against one
   run expectation. This file was only the new stage's because the old shipping stage answered two
   of these differently: it let `::` narrow - `length(xs) :: Int` cutting a sixty-four-bit length on
   one target and being free on the other - and it had no rule at all about a converted value
   filling a position the callee writes through.
-}

fn takesByte(value: I8) -> I8 = value
fn takesIndex(value: Size) -> Size = value
fn writesLong(&value: I64) -> {} = {}

fn narrowing(n: Int) -> Int:
    -- `::` selects a target type and may widen. It does not narrow: an ascription that could remove
    -- data would make it the one construct in the language that does so silently.
    let cut = n :: I8

    -- The same rule at a position nobody wrote a conversion at, which is the other half of one
    -- sentence: an implicit position says what type it wanted and an ascription says what `::` is.
    let called = takesByte(n)

    return 0

{-
   The refusal that is correct and surprising in the same breath - a width the *target* picks.

   On the only native machine there is, `Size` is a signed sixty-four-bit integer and a `U32` fits it
   twice over. What decides the ladder is the *bound*: `Size` is whatever word the target chose, so
   the language guarantees thirty-two bits, which is thirty-one positive ones, and a `U32` needs
   thirty-two. `explainAbstractWidth` is the note that follows, and it is asserted here because the
   general message answers the wrong question on its own.

   `Int` into `Size` is the widening beside it and is accepted - every value of a signed thirty-two
   holds in a signed word of at least that width, on every target - which is what makes this a
   statement about signedness rather than about abstract widths being unreachable.
-}
fn abstractWidth(u: UInt, i: Int) -> Size:
    let ok = takesIndex(i)
    return takesIndex(u)

{-
   A position the callee **writes through**, which a converted value cannot fill.

   Widening the local would make eight fresh bytes of this frame, the callee would write into those,
   and `n` would never see what it wrote. The conversion exists - `Widen(I32, I64)` is a rung - so
   the ordinary "must be I64, and this is I32" would send an author to add a widening they cannot
   have, and the message names the storage instead.
-}
fn writtenThrough() -> Int:
    let &n = 0 :: Int
    writesLong(n)
    return n

-- And a pair no instance relates at all, which is the ordinary answer and the one almost every
-- reader of these messages is looking at. `Bool` is a declared enum and joins no numeric ladder.
fn unrelated(n: Int) -> Int:
    let b = n :: Bool
    return 0

fn main() -> Int = narrowing(1) + writtenThrough() + unrelated(2)
